[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering Panic, Recover, and Defers in Go"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Error handling in Go is traditionally done using explicit return values of type error. However, Go also provides a mechanism for handling runtime anomalies through panic, recover, and defer. These constructs are designed for exceptional situations where a function cannot continue execution."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding defer"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The defer keyword schedules a function call to be run immediately before the function containing the deferred call returns. Defers are commonly used for cleanup actions such as closing files, unlocking mutexes, or closing database connections."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func main() {
    defer fmt.Println("World")
    fmt.Println("Hello")
}"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Deferred functions are executed in LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) order."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Arguments to a deferred function are evaluated when the defer statement is evaluated, not when the function executes."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Handling Anomalies with panic"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"A panic is a built-in function that stops the ordinary flow of control and begins panicking. When a function F calls panic, execution of F stops, any deferred functions in F are executed normally, and then F returns to its caller. To the caller, F then acts as a call to panic."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"package main

func riskyOperation() {
    panic("something went severely wrong")
}

func main() {
    riskyOperation()
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Regaining Control with recover"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"Recover is a built-in function that regains control of a panicking goroutine. Recover is only useful inside deferred functions. During normal execution, a call to recover will return nil and have no other effect. If the goroutine is panicking, a call to recover will capture the value given to panic and resume normal execution."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"package main

import "fmt"

func safeDivision() {
    defer func() {
        if r := recover(); r != nil {
            fmt.Println("Recovered from panic:", r)
        }
    }()

    fmt.Println("About to divide...")
    var a = 10
    var b = 0
    _ = a / b
    fmt.Println("Division completed successfully.")
}

func main() {
    safeDivision()
    fmt.Println("Program continues normally after recovery.")
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices"},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use standard error returns for expected failure conditions instead of panicking."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Limit panic and recover to truly exceptional circumstances, such as unrecoverable startup failures."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always place the recover call inside a deferred function to ensure it executes when a panic unwinds the stack."}]
